WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:06.700
There’s two things here, the main topic,
and the meta topic.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.970
The main topic is a neat algorithm for solving
many two-dimensional equations.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:15.500
That is, equations with two unknown real numbers,
or perhaps those involving a single unknown

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.409
which is a complex number.

00:00:17.409 --> 00:00:22.570
So for example, if you want to find the complex
roots of a polynomial, or some of those million

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.089
dollar zeros of the Riemann zeta function,
this algorithm will do it for you.

00:00:27.089 --> 00:00:32.349
This method can be super pretty, since there
is a lot of color involved, and the core underlying

00:00:32.349 --> 00:00:37.690
idea applies to all sorts of math beyond this
algorithm for solving equations, including

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:40.780
a bit of topology, which I’ll talk about
afterwards.

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:44.890
But what really makes this worth the 20 minutes
or so of your time is that it illustrates

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:49.910
a lesson much more generally useful throughout
math, which is to try to define constructs

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.079
which compose nicely with each other.

00:00:52.079 --> 00:00:55.390
You’ll see what I mean by that as the story
progresses.

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.789
To motivate the case with functions that have
2d inputs and 2d outputs, let’s start off

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:05.120
simpler, with functions that just take in
a real number and spit out a real number.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:10.230
If you want to know when one function, f(x),
equals another function, g(x), you might think

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:14.260
of this as searching for when the graphs of
these functions intersect.

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.260
Right?

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:19.330
That tells you the input where both functions
have the same output.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:25.490
To take a very simple example, say f(x) is
x2, and g(x) is the constant function 2.

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.620
In other words, you want to find the square
root of 2.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:33.340
Even if you know almost nothing about finding
square roots, you probably see quickly that

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:40.111
12 is less than 2 and 22 is bigger than 2,
and so you realize “Ah, there’s a solution

00:01:40.111 --> 00:01:42.310
somewhere between 1 and 2”.

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:47.080
And then if you wanted to narrow it down further,
you might try squaring the halfway point,

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:48.080
1.5.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:53.050
This comes out at 2.25, a bit too high, so
now you focus on the region between 1 and

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.930
1.5.

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:55.930
And so on.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:05.530
You can keep computing at the midpoint and
chopping your search space in half.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:09.660
Another way to think about this, which will
make it easier once we get up to higher dimensions,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.050
is to instead focus on the equivalent question
of when the difference between the two functions

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:17.250
is 0.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:23.060
We found a region of inputs where this was
negative at one end, and positive at the other.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:28.980
And then we split this region in two, narrowing
our attention to a half whose outermost points

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.110
again produced varying signs.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:35.850
We were able to keep going forever in this
way, taking each region with varying signs

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:40.980
on its border and finding a smaller such region
among its halves, knowing that ultimately,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:46.150
we had to be narrowing in on a point where
we would hit exactly zero.

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:50.460
In short, solving equations can always be
framed as finding when a certain function

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.170
is 0.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:55.970
And to do that, use the we use the heuristic
“If f is positive at one point, and f is

00:02:55.970 --> 00:03:00.210
negative at another point, then we can find
some place in between where it’s zero (…at

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:03.950
least, if everything changes smoothly, with
no sudden jumps)”.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:08.970
The amazing thing I want to show you is how
to extend this kind of thinking to two-dimensional

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:14.910
equations; to equations between functions
whose inputs and outputs are both two-dimensional.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.200
For example, complex numbers are two-dimensional,
and the tool we develop here is perfect for

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.020
finding solutions to complex equations.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.849
Since we’re going to be discussing 2d functions
so much,, let’s take a brief side-step to

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:29.360
consider how we illustrate them.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:35.370
Graphing functions with 2d inputs and 2d outputs
would require 4 dimensions, which won’t

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:40.810
work so well in our 3d world on our 2d screens,
but we still have a few good options.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:45.990
One is just to look at at both the input and
output space side-by-side.

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:51.319
Each point in the input space moves to a particular
point in the output space, and I can show

00:03:51.319 --> 00:03:57.050
how moving the input point corresponds to
certain movements in the output space.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:03.590
All the functions we consider will be continuous,
in the sense that small changes in input cause

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:07.440
small changes in output, without any sudden
jumps.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.380
Another option is to think of the arrow from
the origin of the output space to the output

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.370
point, and attach a miniature version of that
arrow to the input point.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:22.900
This can give us a sense at a glance for where
a given input point goes, or where many different

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.059
input points go by drawing a full vector field.

00:04:27.059 --> 00:04:32.509
(Unfortunately, this can also be a bit cluttered;
here, let me make all these arrows the same

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:37.889
size, so we can more easily get a sense of
just the direction of the output at each point.)

00:04:37.889 --> 00:04:42.130
But perhaps the prettiest way to illustrate
2d functions, and the one we’ll use most

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:46.939
in this video, is to associate each point
in the output space with a color.

00:04:46.939 --> 00:04:50.789
Here, we’ve used different “hues” (that
is, where the color falls along a rainbow

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:56.189
or color wheel) to correspond to the direction
away from the origin, while we’ve used darkness

00:04:56.189 --> 00:05:00.060
or brightness to correspond to distance from
the origin.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.550
For example, focusing just on this ray of
outputs, all these points are red, but the

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:10.340
ones closer to the origin are a little darker
and the ones further away are a little lighter.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:15.360
And focusing just on this ray of outputs,
all the points are green, and again, closer

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.949
to the origin we get darker and further away
we get lighter.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:25.560
And so on, we’ve just assigned a color to
each direction, all changing continuously.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:29.919
(The darkness and brightness differences here
can be quite subtle, but for this video all

00:05:29.919 --> 00:05:34.229
we will care about is the directions of outputs,
and not their magnitudes; the hue, not the

00:05:34.229 --> 00:05:35.289
brightness.

00:05:35.289 --> 00:05:39.669
The only important thing about brightness
is just that near the origin, which has no

00:05:39.669 --> 00:05:42.870
particular direction, our colors will fade
to black.)

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:51.469
Now that we’ve decided on colors for each
output, we can visualize 2d functions by coloring

00:05:51.469 --> 00:05:56.669
each point in the input space based on the
color of the point where it lands in the output

00:05:56.669 --> 00:05:57.669
space.

00:05:57.669 --> 00:06:04.270
I like to imagine many points from the input
space hopping over to the output space, which

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:08.729
is basically one big color wheel now, getting
painted, and then hopping back to where they

00:06:08.729 --> 00:06:11.960
came from.

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:17.640
This gives a way, just by looking at the input
space, to understand roughly where the function

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.150
takes each point.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:24.020
For example, this stripe of pink points on
the left tells us that all those points get

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:30.349
mapped to the pink direction, the lower left
of the output space.

00:06:30.349 --> 00:06:34.219
Those three points which are black with lots
of colors around them are the ones that go

00:06:34.219 --> 00:06:37.470
to zero.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:44.740
Alright, so just like the 1d case, solving
an equation of two-dimensional equations can

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:49.740
always be reframed as asking when a certain
function equals 0.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:54.889
So that’s our challenge right now: Create
an algorithm that finds which input points

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:58.849
a given 2d function takes to 0.

00:06:58.849 --> 00:07:04.289
Now, you might point out that if you’re
looking at a color map like this, by seeing

00:07:04.289 --> 00:07:10.539
these black dots you know where the zeros
of the function are...so, does that count?

00:07:10.539 --> 00:07:15.779
Well, keep in mind that to create this diagram
we’ve had the computer compute the function

00:07:15.779 --> 00:07:17.490
at all these pixels on the plane.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:22.240
But part of our goal here is to find a more
efficient algorithm that only requires computing

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:29.550
the function on as few points as possible;
only having limited view of the colors of

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.550
the plane, so to speak.

00:07:30.550 --> 00:07:33.710
Also, from a more theoretical standpoint,
it’d be nice to have a general construct

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:38.279
which gives us conditions for whether or not
a zero even exists inside a given region.

00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:43.669
Now, in 1-dimension, the main insight was
that if a continuous function is positive

00:07:43.669 --> 00:07:48.229
at one point, and negative at another, then
somewhere in between it must be 0.

00:07:48.229 --> 00:07:50.729
How do you extend to two dimensions?

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:54.199
You need some analog of talking about signs.

00:07:54.199 --> 00:08:00.219
Well, one way to think about what signs are
is as directions; positive means pointing

00:08:00.219 --> 00:08:04.659
right along a number line, and negative means
pointing left along that number line.

00:08:04.659 --> 00:08:08.699
Two-dimensional quantities also have directions,
although for them, the options are wider:

00:08:08.699 --> 00:08:12.749
they can point anywhere along a whole circle
of possibilities.

00:08:12.749 --> 00:08:16.389
So in the same way that for 1d functions,
we were asking whether a given function was

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:22.300
positive or negative on the boundary of a
range, which is just two points; for 2d functions

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.210
we will want to look at the boundary of a
region, which will be some loop, and ask about

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:33.719
the direction of the function’s output along
that boundary.

00:08:33.719 --> 00:08:39.599
For example, we see that on this loop around
this zero, the output goes through every possible

00:08:39.599 --> 00:08:43.430
direction, all the colors of the rainbow;
red, yellow, green, blue, back to red, and

00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:45.779
everything in between along the way.

00:08:45.779 --> 00:08:50.810
But on this loop over here, with no zeros
inside, the output doesn’t go through every

00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:55.399
color; it only goes through some orangish
colors, but never, say, green or blue.

00:08:55.399 --> 00:08:59.080
This is promising; it looks a lot like how
things worked in 1d.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:03.510
Maybe, in the same way that if a 1d function
takes both possible signs on the boundary

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:09.680
of a 1d region, there’s a zero somewhere
between, we might hypothesize that if a 2d

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:15.310
function hits outputs of all possible directions,
all possible colors, on the boundary of a

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:21.069
2d region, somewhere inside that region it
must go to zero.

00:09:21.069 --> 00:09:27.650
Here, take a moment to really think about
if this should be true, and if so, why.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:32.010
If we start by thinking about a tiny loop
around some input point, we know, since everything

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:36.500
is continuous, that our function takes it
to some tiny loop near the corresponding output.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:41.149
But look: most tiny loops of outputs barely
vary in color.

00:09:41.149 --> 00:09:46.300
If you pick any output point other than zero,
and draw a sufficiently tight loop near it,

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:49.860
the loop’s colors will all be about the
same as the color of that point.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:55.190
A tight loop here will be all blue-ish; a
tight loop here will be all yellow-ish, etc.

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:58.440
You certainly won’t get every color of the
rainbow.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:02.709
The only point you can tighten loops around
while still going through all the colors of

00:10:02.709 --> 00:10:07.110
the rainbow is the colorless origin: zero
itself.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:11.579
So it is indeed the case that if you have
loops going through every color of the rainbow,

00:10:11.579 --> 00:10:15.970
tightening and tightening and narrowing in
on a point, then that point must in fact be

00:10:15.970 --> 00:10:16.970
a zero.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:22.331
…And so we can set up our 2d equation solver
just like our 1d equation solver: when we

00:10:22.331 --> 00:10:27.509
find a large region whose border goes through
every color, split it in two, and look at

00:10:27.509 --> 00:10:30.190
the colors on the boundary on each half.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:34.930
In the example shown here, the border of the
left half doesn’t go through all colors;

00:10:34.930 --> 00:10:40.579
there are no points that map to the orange
and yellow directions, so we grey this area

00:10:40.579 --> 00:10:43.440
out as a way of saying we don’t want to
search it further.

00:10:43.440 --> 00:10:47.930
The right half does go through all colors,
spending a lot of time in the green direction,

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:50.050
then passing yellow-orange-red, as well as
blue-violet.

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:57.800
Remember, that means points of this boundary
get mapped to outputs of all possible directions,

00:10:57.800 --> 00:11:01.162
so we’ll explore it further, subdividing
again and checking the boundary colors of

00:11:01.162 --> 00:11:02.769
each subregion.

00:11:02.769 --> 00:11:07.560
The boundary of that top right is all green,
so we’ll stop searching there, but the bottom

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:10.490
is colorful enough to deserve a subdivision.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.430
And just continue like this!

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:17.230
Check which subregion has a boundary covering
all colors, meaning points of that boundary

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:21.279
get mapped to all possible directions, and
keep chopping those subregions in half like

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:23.319
we did in the 1d case.

00:11:23.319 --> 00:11:27.910
...Except...wait a minute...what happened
here?

00:11:27.910 --> 00:11:34.220
Neither of those last subdivisions on the
bottom right passes through all colors...so

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:43.959
our algorithm stopped without having found
a zero…

00:11:43.959 --> 00:11:47.850
So being wrong is a regular part of doing
math.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:53.860
We had this hypothesis that led us to a proposed
algorithm, and clearly we were mistaken somewhere.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:57.520
Being good at math is not about being right
the first time, it’s about having the resilience

00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:02.480
to carefully look back and understand our
mistakes, and how to fix them.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:06.741
The problem here was that we had a region
whose border went through every color, but

00:12:06.741 --> 00:12:10.819
when we split it down the middle, neither
subregion’s border went through every color.

00:12:10.819 --> 00:12:15.269
We had no options for where to keep searching
next, breaking our zero-finder.

00:12:15.269 --> 00:12:20.209
In 1d, this sort of thing never happened:
any time you had an interval whose endpoints

00:12:20.209 --> 00:12:25.990
had different signs, when you split it, you
knew you were guaranteed to get some sub-interval

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:29.329
whose endpoints still had different-signs.

00:12:29.329 --> 00:12:34.079
Put another way, any time you have two intervals
whose endpoints don’t change sign, when

00:12:34.079 --> 00:12:39.160
you combine them, you get a bigger interval
whose endpoints don’t change sign.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:46.320
But in 2d, you can take two regions whose
borders don’t contain every color, and combine

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:49.920
them into a region whose border DOES contain
every color.

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:54.410
And in just this way, our proposed zero-finder
can break down.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:13:01.040
In fact, you can have a big loop whose border
goes through every color, without there being

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.339
any zeros inside

00:13:04.339 --> 00:13:08.420
We weren’t wrong in our claims about tiny
loops; when we said that forever-narrowing

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:13.019
loops that go through every color had to be
narrowing in on a zero.

00:13:13.019 --> 00:13:17.269
But what made a mess for us is that, the “Does
my border go through every color or not?”

00:13:17.269 --> 00:13:22.060
property doesn’t combine in a nice, predictable
way when you combine regions.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:23.560
But don’t worry!

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:28.769
It turns out, we can modify this slightly,
to a more sophisticated property that DOES

00:13:28.769 --> 00:13:39.589
combine nicely, giving us what we want.

00:13:39.589 --> 00:13:43.819
Instead of simply asking whether we find every
color at some point along a loop, let’s

00:13:43.819 --> 00:13:48.620
keep track more carefully of how those colors
change as as we walk along the loop.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:50.140
Let me show what you I mean with some examples.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:54.120
I’ll keep a little color wheel here in the
corner to help us keep track.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:59.840
When colors along a path of inputs move through
the rainbow from red to yellow, or yellow

00:13:59.840 --> 00:14:05.720
to green, green to blue, or blue to red, the
output is swinging clockwise.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:10.290
On the other hand, when the colors move the
other way through the rainbow, from blue to

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:17.170
green, green to yellow, yellow to red, or
red to blue, the output is swinging counterclockwise.

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:22.949
So walking along this short path here, the
colors wind a fifth of the way clockwise through

00:14:22.949 --> 00:14:24.440
the color wheel.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:29.630
And walking along this path here, the colors
wind another fifth of the way clockwise through

00:14:29.630 --> 00:14:30.630
the color wheel.

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:35.389
Of course, this means that if we go through
both paths, one after another, the colors

00:14:35.389 --> 00:14:39.370
wind a total of two-fifths of a full turn
clockwise.

00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:44.930
The total amount of winding just adds up;
this is the kind of straightforward combining

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:47.129
that will be useful to us.

00:14:47.129 --> 00:14:51.850
When I say “total amount of winding”,
I want you to imagine an old-fashioned odometer

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:58.709
that ticks forward as the arrow spins clockwise,
but ticks backward as the arrow spins counterclockwise.

00:14:58.709 --> 00:15:03.040
So counterclockwise winding counts as negative
clockwise winding.

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:07.449
The output may turn and turn a lot, but if
some of that turning is in opposite directions,

00:15:07.449 --> 00:15:08.940
it cancels out.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:14.160
For example, if you move forward along a path,
and then move backward along that same path,

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:18.079
the total winding number will end up being
zero: the backwards movement will literally

00:15:18.079 --> 00:15:22.930
“re-wind” through all the previously seen
colors, reversing all the previous winding

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:25.749
and returning the odometer to where it started.

00:15:25.749 --> 00:15:30.290
We can look at winding along loops, too.

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:35.980
For example, if we walk around this entire
loop clockwise, the outputs we come across

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:42.029
wind around a total of three full turns clockwise:
the colors swung through the rainbow, in ROYGBIV

00:15:42.029 --> 00:15:47.100
order, from red to red again... and then again...
and again.

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:50.829
In the jargon mathematicians use, we say that
along this loop, the total “winding number”

00:15:50.829 --> 00:15:51.829
is three.

00:15:51.829 --> 00:15:54.100
In the case of this loop, the winding number
was three.

00:15:54.100 --> 00:15:56.870
For other loops it could be any other whole
number.

00:15:56.870 --> 00:16:02.779
Large ones, if the output swings around many
times as the input walks along a loop.

00:16:02.779 --> 00:16:07.019
Smaller ones, if the output only swings around
once or twice.

00:16:07.019 --> 00:16:12.230
Or the winding number could even be a negative
integer, if the output loops around counterclockwise

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.170
as we walk clockwise around the loop.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:22.430
But along any loop, it will be a whole number,
because by the time we return to where we

00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:24.000
started, we have to get back to the same output
we started with.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:25.000
(Along paths that don’t end back where they
start, we can get fractions of turns, as we

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:26.990
saw earlier, but whenever you combine these
into a loop, the total will be a whole number)

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:31.480
Incidentally, if a path actually contains
a point where the output is precisely zero,

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:36.160
then we can’t define the winding number
along it, since the output has no particular

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.520
direction anymore.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.600
This won’t be a problem for us, though.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:44.589
Our whole goal is to find zeros, so if this
ever comes up, we’ve just lucked out early.

00:16:44.589 --> 00:16:55.029
Alright, so winding numbers add up nicely
when you combine paths into bigger paths,

00:16:55.029 --> 00:17:00.249
but what we really want is for winding numbers
around the borders of regions to add up nicely

00:17:00.249 --> 00:17:02.959
when you combine regions into bigger regions.

00:17:02.959 --> 00:17:05.850
Do we have this property?

00:17:05.850 --> 00:17:09.420
Well, take a look!

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:15.530
The winding number as we go clockwise around
this region is the sum of the winding numbers

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:17.920
from these paths.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:22.950
And the winding as we go clockwise around
this region is the sum of the winding numbers

00:17:22.950 --> 00:17:25.660
from these paths.

00:17:25.660 --> 00:17:29.550
When we combine those two regions into this
bigger region, most of those paths become

00:17:29.550 --> 00:17:33.720
part of the clockwise border of the bigger
region.

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:35.620
And as for the parts that don’t?

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:40.650
They cancel out; one is just the other in
reverse, the “re-winding” of the other,

00:17:40.650 --> 00:17:42.630
as we saw before.

00:17:42.630 --> 00:17:48.160
So winding numbers along borders of regions
add up just the way we want them to!

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:54.390
(As a side note, this reasoning about borders
adding up this way comes up a lot in mathematics,

00:17:54.390 --> 00:17:58.750
and is often called “Stokes’ Theorem”;
those of you who have studied multivariable

00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:01.460
calculus may recognize it from that context…)

00:18:01.460 --> 00:18:07.160
And now, finally, with winding numbers in
hand, we can get back to our equation solving

00:18:07.160 --> 00:18:08.160
goals.

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:12.380
The problem with the region we saw earlier
is that even though its border passed through

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:16.580
all colors, the winding number was 0.

00:18:16.580 --> 00:18:22.330
The outputs wound around halfway, through
yellow to red, then started going counterclockwise

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:27.810
back through blue and hitting red from the
other direction, then wound clockwise again

00:18:27.810 --> 00:18:32.000
in such a way that the total winding netted
out to be zero.

00:18:32.000 --> 00:18:42.190
But if you find any loop with a nonzero winding
number and split it in half, at least one

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:47.540
of the halves is guaranteed to have a nonzero
winding number, since things add up nicely.

00:18:47.540 --> 00:18:54.360
And in this way, we can keep going, narrowing
in further and further on a point…

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:59.000
As we narrow in on that point, we’ll be
doing so using tiny loops with nonzero winding

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:02.950
number, which must be tiny loops which go
through every color, and therefore, as we

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.670
discussed before, that point must be a zero.

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:08.720
And that’s it!

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:14.190
We have now created our 2d equation solver,
and this time, I promise, there are no bugs.

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:18.170
“Winding numbers” are precisely the tool
we needed to make this work.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:24.090
We can now solve equations “Where does f(x)
= g(x)?” in 2d just by considering how the

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.770
difference between f and g winds around.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:31.900
Whenever we have a loop whose winding number
isn’t zero, we can run this algorithm on

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:35.090
it, and are guaranteed to find a solution
somewhere within it.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:41.020
And what’s more, just as in 1d, our equation
solver is remarkably efficient; we keep narrowing

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:46.470
in to half the size of our region each round,
thus quickly narrowing in on our zeros, and

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:51.260
all the while, we only have to check the value
of the function along points of these loops,

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:55.210
rather than checking it on all the many, many
points inside.

00:19:55.210 --> 00:20:00.240
So in some sense the overall “work” we
do is proportional only to our search space’s

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.720
perimeter, not its area.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:06.940
Which is amazing!

00:20:06.940 --> 00:20:12.060
It is weirdly mesmerizing to watch this in
action; just giving it some function and letting

00:20:12.060 --> 00:20:13.780
search for zeros.

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:17.990
Like I said before, complex numbers are 2d,
so we can apply this algorithm to equations

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:20.330
between functions from complex numbers to
complex numbers.

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:27.091
For example, here’s our algorithm finding
all the zeros of the function x5 - x - 1 over

00:20:27.091 --> 00:20:35.440
the complex numbers, starting by considering
a very large region around the origin.

00:20:35.440 --> 00:20:40.010
Each time you find a loop with nonzero winding
number, you split it in half, and figure out

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:42.430
the winding numbers of the two smaller loops.

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:47.300
Either one or both of the smaller loops will
have nonzero winding number; when you see

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:51.650
this, you know there is a zero to be found
in that loop, and so you keep going in the

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:53.210
same way in that smaller search space.

00:20:53.210 --> 00:20:54.350
As for loops whose winding number is zero,
you don’t explore those further inside.

00:20:54.350 --> 00:21:00.240
(We also stop exploring a region when we stumble
directly across a zero point inside it, right

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:04.650
on the nose, as happened once on the right
half here; these rare occurrences interfere

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:13.570
with our ability to compute winding numbers,
but hey, we get 

00:21:13.570 --> 00:21:18.520
a zero!).

00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:22.510
And letting our equation solver continue in
this same way, it eventually converges on

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:24.640
lots of zeros of this polynomial.

00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:31.320
Incidentally, it’s no coincidence that the
overall winding number came out to 5 in this

00:21:31.320 --> 00:21:32.320
example.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:40.040
With complex numbers, the operation xn directly
corresponds to winding around n times as we

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:49.160
loop around the origin, and for large enough
inputs, every term in a polynomial other than

00:21:49.160 --> 00:21:52.710
the leading term becomes insignificant in
comparison.

00:21:52.710 --> 00:21:59.420
So any complex polynomial of degree n has
a winding number of n around a large enough

00:21:59.420 --> 00:22:00.420
loop.

00:22:00.420 --> 00:22:05.360
In this way, our winding number technology
actually guarantees us that every complex

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:13.980
polynomial has a zero; mathematicians call
this the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra.

00:22:13.980 --> 00:22:18.050
Having an algorithm for finding numerical
solutions to equations like this is extremely

00:22:18.050 --> 00:22:19.050
practical, but I should say that we’ve left
out a few details on how you’d implement

00:22:19.050 --> 00:22:20.050
it.

00:22:20.050 --> 00:22:21.050
For example, to know how frequently you should
sample points, you’d want to know how quickly

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:22.050
the direction of the output changes, we’ve
left some more details in the description.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.050
But the fundamental theorem of algebra is
a good example of how these winding numbers

00:22:23.050 --> 00:22:28.210
are also quite useful on a theoretical level,
guaranteeing the existence of a zero for a

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:31.530
broad class of functions under suitable conditions.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:41.390
We’ll see a few more amazing applications
of this in a follow-up video, including correcting

